<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hanwant Singh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/1952">1952</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hanwant Singh of Jodhpur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hanwant Singh was the last ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a>, succeeding in 1947. At independence he briefly considered acceding to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> before signing the accession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and in 1949 his state merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>. He entered electoral politics and was killed in an air crash in January 1952, during the first Indian general election.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hanwant-singh.jpg" alt="Hanwant Singh" width="250" height="340" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hanwant_Singh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hanwant Singh was born on 16 June 1923 into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathore dynasty</a> of Marwar, whose kingdom was the third largest of the Indian princely states. In 1943 he married Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-kumari">Krishna Kumari</a> of Dhrangadhra. They had three children: a son, Gaj Singh Rathore, who succeeded him, and two daughters, Sailesh Kumari, who married into Banswara, and Chandresh Kumari Katoch of Kangra.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He succeeded his father as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> on 9 June 1947 and held the title until his death. In 1948 he met Sandra McBryde, a 19-year-old Scottish nurse, with whom he had a brief affair. He later married the Muslim actress Zubeida, who converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and took the name Vidya Rani; they had a son, Hukum Singh Rathore, known as Tutu Bana. The marriage drew a rebuke from the royal family, and he went to live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehrangarh">Mehrangarh</a>.</p><p>After independence and the formation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> as a state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, he founded a new political party, the Akhil Bhartiya Ramrajya Parishad, in 1952. He campaigned for his party's candidates in both the Indian general election and the state Assembly elections, which were scheduled for February 1952.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 26 January 1952, after only four hours of sleep, he took off in a small aircraft with Zubeida. The plane crashed near Sumerpur, killing them both; he was 28. Their son Tutu was brought up by Rajmata Krishna Kumari and studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayo-college">Mayo College</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>. He married Rao Rani Rajeshwari Kumari Rathore, a daughter of Rao Raja Daljit Singh of Alwar, and was found beheaded on the streets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> on 17 April 1981.</p><p>In 2011 the wreckage of the aircraft, a Beechcraft Bonanza, a light six-seater, was discovered inside Jodhpur Central Jail. In 2012 the Mehrangarh Fort Museum, which Maharaja Gaj Singh had founded in 1972, formally asked the jail to transfer the wreckage to the museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hanwant Singh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1947 Maharaja <b>Hanwant Singh</b> briefly considered joining Pakistan before acceding to India, and in 1949 Jodhpur merged into Greater Rajasthan.</blockquote><a href="/state/jodhpur-state">Jodhpur State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.29,73.03&amp;year=1941" aria-label="Jodhpur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6478 1198 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hanwant Singh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6928 1468h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6928 1468h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.29,73.03&amp;year=1941">What was here in 1941? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
